# `Unit(T)`: a value that only one unit's tasks reach, one task at a time.
# SPEC.md III.4.18. A unit is a set of tasks of which at most one runs at
# any instant and which switch only where one parks; a `Unit` is one you
# make and give tasks to, with a value it owns that no other code reaches.
# It is the actor without the mailbox: the tasks are the messages.
#
#     import std/unit::{Unit}
#
#     counts = Unit.new { {} of String => Int32 }
#     group do |g|
#       words.each { |w| g.spawn(in: counts) { |hits| hits[w] = (hits[w]? || 0) + 1; 0 } }
#     end
#     total = 0
#     group do |g|
#       size = g.spawn(in: counts) { |hits| hits.size }
#       total = size.value.or(0)
#     end
#
# **A task of the group, in the Unit's unit.** `g.spawn(in: u) { |value| }`
# is a task of `g`, joined, cancelled and failed by `g`'s scope as every
# task is (III.4.1-III.4.3). It runs in `u`'s unit and is handed the value
# `u` owns: never at the same instant as another of `u`'s tasks, and on a
# pool of more than one worker on whichever worker is free. A task it
# spawns with `unit: :same`, or a plain `spawn` the compiler keeps in its
# unit, is in `u`'s unit too and may share the value. With one worker
# (`IYI_WORKERS=1`) every task of every `Unit` runs on the main thread,
# whichever thread spawned it, so one worker is one thread.
#
# **Tasks interleave where they park.** One task of a `Unit` that parks -
# a channel's receive, a `sleep`, a join, a lock - lets another of its
# tasks run before it resumes, as unit-mates always may; Swift's actors
# do the same. A value read before a park may have changed after it, so
# what must hold of the value holds at every park, not only at a task's
# end:
#
#     g.spawn(in: account) do |a|
#       seen = a.balance
#       sleep(1)                   # another task of `account` may run here
#       a.balance = seen - 10      # wrong: write `a.balance -= 10` after the park
#       0
#     end
#
# **Only the Unit's tasks reach the value, and the compiler checks every
# block.** The value is made by the block `Unit.new` runs, so nothing
# outside holds it, and is handed only to the blocks `spawn(in:)` runs.
# Those blocks may capture only `Share` values - a captured array would be
# within reach of the value and of another unit at once - and what such a
# task answers must be `Share`, since it leaves the unit while the unit
# lives on. A block that breaks either is refused where it is written,
# naming the variable or the answer (`CleanupTransformer#check_share_boundary`).
# So `Unit(T)` is `Share` for any `T`, as `Mutex(T)` is: a `Unit` may be
# captured by any task, thread or `Unit` block. What the check does not see
# is a method the block calls that stores the value somewhere global; that
# is the same edge `Mutex` and the thread check have.
#
# **A panic releases the Unit.** A task of a `Unit` that panics dies alone,
# as any task does (III.1.4); the `Unit` is not poisoned, and its next
# task runs. What the panicking task changed of the value stays changed.
#
# **The value is handed by what it is.** A class, an `Array`, a `Hash` is
# handed by reference, so a task's change is the next task's value. A
# struct - an `Int32`, a `Tuple` - is handed as a copy, so own a class
# that holds it when tasks must change it.

@[Unsafe("a Unit is trusted to cross tasks under @[Share(owns: true)] because only its unit's tasks reach the value, one at a time")]
module std/unit

# A value that only one unit's tasks reach, one task at a time: the block
# `Unit.new` runs makes it, and `g.spawn(in: u) { |value| }` runs a task of
# `g` in its unit, handed it (the header above; SPEC.md III.4.18).
@[Share(owns: true)]
pub class Unit(T) < IyiGroup::Unit(T)
  # A `Unit` owning the value *block* makes. The block may capture only
  # `Share` values: it is the value's way in (`CleanupTransformer#check_share_boundary`).
  #
  # With `-Dheap_partitions` (heap22 row 4) the block's captures are
  # published, the unit and its partition are made first, the value is
  # made in that partition, and the handle, which any unit may hold, in
  # the shared heap. *pinned* is the compiler's, as `spawn_new`'s is.
  def self.new(*, pinned : Bool = false, &block : -> U) : Unit(U) forall U
    # Named outside the `{% if %}`: a block parameter read only inside one
    # is not captured.
    IyiCross.publish(block)
    {% if flag?(:heap_partitions) %}
      placed = IyiWorkers.parallel? ? POOL : MAIN
      unit = IyiCross.shared { IyiUnit.new(pinned: pinned || placed == MAIN) }
      value = IyiHeap.within(unit.heap) { block.call }
      handle = IyiCross.shared { Unit(U).build(value, unit, placed) }
      # The handle's value is a root of the unit's partition (R(u)).
      unit.value_slot = pointerof(handle.@value).address
      handle
    {% else %}
      Unit(U).build(block.call)
    {% end %}
  end

  protected def self.build(value : T) : Unit(T)
    new(value)
  end

  protected def self.build(value : T, unit : IyiUnit, placed : UInt64) : Unit(T)
    new(value, unit, placed)
  end

  private def initialize(value : T)
    super(value)
  end

  private def initialize(value : T, unit : IyiUnit, placed : UInt64)
    super(value, unit, placed)
  end
end
